\page guided_tour Guided tour  

[TOC]

Introduction         {#gt_introduction}
============ 

Throughout this guided tour, we will walk you through the the most common VEF features. 
This guided tour is written for the \ref page_expert_mode "expert mode" that provides the
most elaborate control over the virtual experiments.


Expert mode - what's that?
--------------------------

The VEF consists of a number of programs. One of these, the `alamDMC` program, is the corner stone, since it carries out most of the computational work. In this guided tour we will primarily demonstrate how to use `alamDMC` and how to interpret its results. We call it "expert mode" because it comes down to writing configuration files and running programs from a command line (command prompt on Windows or user shell on Linux). 


Let's start!
------------

Before we start, please copy the content of directory `guided_tour` from the 
VEF installation directory to another directory on your computer. We will refer
to that copy as `guided_tour_directory`.

Please run the VEF command prompt window and change the directory to `guided_tour_directory`.


Input texture        {#gt_input_texture}
=============

The VEF calculations require crystallographic texture data as input. The texture 
is usually provided as the Orientation Distribution Function (ODF), either in the form of 
a continuous function or as OFD intensities on a regular grid. Before we use the texture data 
in the VEF software, we have to make a discrete ODF by sampling orientations from the ODF  
to get a collection of discrete orientations, which as a whole approximates the input ODF.
In this example we are going to start from the ODF exported from MTEX Toolbox \opcite{MTEX}.

\note The VEF currently supports importing texture data from MTEX.  
However,the data must satisfy two requirements: triclinic symmetry must  
be pre-imposed and Bunge convention has to be used. 
Please see 
<a href=https://mtex-toolbox.github.io/files/doc/ODFImportExport.html#7>MTEX documentation</a> 
how to export ODF data to generic ASCII files. 

Let us use the texture data for a ferritic steel (it's DX51D, for your curiosity), given in the file `sid1687f.odf`. It so happens the texture is expressed in the [material reference frame](@ref convention_reference_frame) where the RD, TD and ND are the first, second and third axis, respectively. By the way, this reference frame adheres to a pretty common convention used in measuring texture of rolled alloys.
If you wish, you can first inspect the texture by plotting a set of ODF sections.
To do so, run the command [`odfplot`](@ref odfplot):
<pre class="consoleView">
odfplot --input sid1687f.odf --sections 0,45 --style=filled --no-labels
</pre>

This will produce two PNG files: `sid1687f_0.png` and `sid1687f_45.png`:
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
		<img class="fullsize widefigure" src="sid1687f_0.png" alt="ODF section phi_2 = 0">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">ODF section \f$ \phi_2 = 0^\circ \f$</div>
</div>
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
		<img class="fullsize widefigure" src="sid1687f_45.png" alt="ODF section phi_2 = 0">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">ODF section \f$ \phi_2 = 45^\circ \f$</div>
</div>

As we can see from these ODF sections, the material has a prevalently strong \f$\gamma\f$-fiber.

Let us now sample the ODF to get representative crystal orientations. As a rule of thumb, 
we recommend to use at least 2000 (ideally: 3000 - 7000) orientations to get a good representation 
of the macrotexture. The command [`odf2smt`](@ref odf2smt) will do the sampling:
<pre class="consoleView">
odf2smt --input sid1687f.odf --ncrystals 3000
</pre>
The command will produce a single file `sid1687f.smt` that contains 3000 discrete orientations. 
We will use this file will as input in all the examples in this tutorial.



Anisotropy of the as-received material        {#gt_anisotropy}
======================================

Uniaxial tension   {#gt_QRS}
----------------

The VEF can calculate these quantities for any direction of uniaxial loading. If the undeformed material is considered, the QRS module of `alamDMC` is the most handy way to do so. 

To run the `alamDMC` QRS module, we need an input file that instructs the program what to do (see [reference manual](@ref module_qrs) for full details what can be specified in QRS configuration files)). We have drafted one for you: please find file `sid1687f_qrs.cfg` in `guided_tour_directory`. Let's quickly inspect what's inside:

\includelineno sid1687f_qrs.cfg

As you certainly noticed, there are lines that start with `#`: these are [comments](@ref desc_alamdmc_comments) that you can put in the configuration files. The comments are ignored by the program, but help us to understand the content of the file.

The file has a [fixed format](@ref desc_alamdmc_format), which means the order and the position of the items matter. Lines 1 - 12 contain general settings, while lines 14 - 18 are specific for the QRS module.

Here's what the configuration file tells the `alamDMC`:
- the names of output files will stem from `sid1687f_qrs` (line 4). 
  In our case the primary output file will be named `sid1687f_qrs.xqrs`.  
- If available, the deformed material configuration should be written out to the output files. (line 5)
- Much details about the calculations performed will be [printed to the terminal](@ref verbosity) (line 6)
- [texture file](@ref texture_file) `sid1687f.smt` [we created earlier](#gt_input_texture) will be used as the input (line 7)
- The ALAMEL [crystal plasticity model](@ref model_type) will be used (line 8)
- The crystal plasticity model will assume [bcc crystal structure with 24 slip systems](@ref slip_system_family) (line 9).
- lines 10 - 12 instruct the program to use the defaults for [microstructural description](@ref microstructure_default), [hardening](@ref hardening_default) and [solver settings](@ref solver_default).

The QRS-specific lines mean the following:
- lines 16 - 17 provide the program with the orientations of virtual samples with respect to the [reference frame](@ref convention_reference_frame). This is done by a [range specification](@ref range_option). In this example the orientations are given as a [uniform range](@ref option_uniform) of angles between the loading direction and selected material axis (see below).
- Default settings of uniaxial tension will be used (line 18). These include, among other things, that the reference frame will be given by the material reference frame, in this case RD, TD and ND.  Therefore, the orientations of the virtual tensile samples are given as the angle between RD and the loading direction within the RD-TD plane.

Let's now run the `alamDMC`  program in the command line. The program takes two arguments: name of the module `QRS` and path to the configuration file `sid1687f_qrs.cfg`: 
<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC QRS sid1687f_qrs.cfg
</pre>

The program will place its output in the file `sid1687f_qrs.xqrs`. Let's have a look at 
the leading 5 lines of that file:
\dontinclude expert/sid1687f_qrs.xqrs
\until 0.100000000E+02

The initial two lines constitute a header. The first line that starts with '#' character contains column numbers. Because of the leading '#', the line is typically neglected by upstream analysis software (e.g. plotting program). The second line contains the column names.
You will see headers similar to this one in many output files produced by the VEF.
  
Let's plot the Lankford coefficients (column `r-value` as the function of loading direction (column `angle`):

<div class="figure">
	<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_rvalues.png" alt="Lankford coefficients at different loading directions">
	<div class="figcaption">Lankford coefficients in different directions of uniaxial loading</div>
</div>

By convention the r-values and the yield stress are determined at 20% elongation, whereas the VEF QRS calculates the r-values of the undeformed material, which in our case has 0% elongation. We will come back to this point in [a further part of this guided tour](#gt_udsa).

The column `sigma_xx` contains the tensile stresses. Let's plot this output as well:
<div class="figure">
	<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_tensile_stress.png" alt="Tensile stress at different  loading directions">
	<div class="figcaption">Tensile stress in different directions of uniaxial loading</div>
</div>

The unit for stresses we put in the figure is referring to the fact we do not use any hardening and we make no assumptions if the stress is given in any particular unit. Again, [a later part of this guided tour](#gt_udsa) will shed some light on this issue. 


Yield locus sections    {#gt_YLD}
--------------------

The VEF includes a module that calculates yield locus sections, which is called `YLD`.  

Here is how a typical [configuration file of YLD](@ref configuration_yld) looks like (please see the file `sid1687f_yld.cfg`):
\includelineno sid1687f_yld.cfg

As we see, lines 1 - 12 are identical as in the previous section. The YLD uses the angle \f$\theta\f$ to choose which points on the locus section should be calculated. Figure below gives an illustration how \f$\theta\f$ is defined.  

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="yld_beta_illustration.png" alt="yield locus section: angles theta and beta">
<div class="figcaption">Illustration of \f$\theta\f$ angle that YLD uses to select points in yield locus section. The \f$\beta\f$ angle is one of the outputs from the YLD module.</div>
</div>

In this example we specify a [multi-biased range](@ref option_multibiased) of \f$\theta\f$ angles, which allows us to probe the yield locus more densely in regions where the curvature of the section is expected to be high, and far more coarsely elsewhere. In our example, the denser scan of the yield locus will be around equibiaxial tension point (i.e. \f$\theta = 45^\circ \f$) and equibiaxial compression point (\f$\theta = 205^\circ \f$). However, the equibiaxial tension point will be covered by more points (see the subranges 1 and 4) than the exquibiaxial compression (see the subranges 2 and 3).

The last line requests to use the defaults, which are: 
- the yield locus section is defined by \f$\sigma_{11} \f$ (normal stress along RD) and \f$\sigma_{22} \f$ (normal stress along TD) directions,
- the yield locus will be scaled by yield stress corresponding to uniaxial tension along the RD.

<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC YLD sid1687f_yld.cfg
</pre>

We may now quickly inspect the content of the output file `sid1687f_yld.xyld` (the file contains 12 columns, so depending on the size of your screen some lines may fold). The top five lines will look similar to this:
\dontinclude expert/sid1687f_yld.xyld
\until   0.50769661E+02

The column `theta` contains the angles that were used for computing the yield locus section. The columns `sigma` and `sigma/sigma_0` include the points on the section, both in polar coordinate system of the angle `theta`. However, we are going to use use the columns `sigma_x` and `sigma_y` that contain the yield locus contour in Cartesian coordinate system.

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_yld.png" alt="yield locus section (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Yield locus section (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

Additionally, we can use columns `dsigma_x` and `dsigma_y` to plot yield locus' normals (actually: the projections of yield locus normals onto the current section of stress space):

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_yld_normals.png" alt="normals to the yield locus section (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Normals to the yield locus section (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

\note The normals to the yield locus contour are approximated numerically by considering also the neighboring points. To ensure good quality of the normals, make sure that the angular resolution of \f$\theta\f$ does not exceed \f$ \approx 5^\circ \f$ in the regions of where the yield locus contour has high curvature. 


Of course, we don't always need very detailed calculations of the yield locus. If just a few points are enough, we can easily get this done. Let's modify our example: please see the changes in lines 4, 16 and 17 in the example below:

\includelineno sid1687f_coarse_yld.cfg

We can see that \f$\theta\f$ is restricted to the range from 0&deg; to 90&deg;  every 15&deg;, so only 7 points will be calculated:
<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_coarse_yld.png" alt="yield locus section, coarse (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Yield locus section with limited \f$\theta\f$ range (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>



Anisotropy evolution due to deformation     {#gt_anisotropy_evol}
=======================================

So far we have used the VEF to derive plastic properties of the __material in a given state__. Now we are going to get predictions how the material state evolves, and at the same time we will see how the properties evolve with strain.


Deformation under uniaxial stress state    {#gt_udsa}
---------------------------------------

In the next step of this tour we will simulate uniaxial tension test. The [VEF module `UDSA`](@ref module_udsa)  provides a virtual uniaxial test. We will track the evolution of the material state up to a plastic strain of approximately 40%, which is much higher than what may usually be realized in an experimental tensile test setup.

Let's take the configuration file `sid1687f_udsa.cfg` that we are about to feed to the UDSA module:
\includelineno sid1687f_udsa.cfg

Again, we made no change in the generic configuration section. The module-specific part tells the VEF to simulate a process where the virtual samples are oriented in-plane of the sheet, and  "cut out" in the directions 0&deg;, 45&deg; and 90&deg; with respect to the RD. The program will simulate tension up to tensile strain 0.4. The total deformation will be subdivided into increments of magnitude 0.01. The option `StrainTensorComponent ` instructs the program to calculate the increments in a way that the tensile strain component will uniformly increase.

Let's run the program:
<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC UDSA sid1687f_udsa.cfg
</pre>

You will see a couple of output files produced this time, in particular `sid1687f_udsa_0_000.uds`, `sid1687f_udsa_45_000.uds` and `sid1687f_udsa_90_000.uds`. As you probably guessed from their names, the files correspond to the three directions of the virtual samples.
We can quickly inspect the content of these files, for instance `sid1687f_udsa_0_000.uds`. The file contains a number of columns. The first one gives the increment number - as we can see from it, the program performed 41 increments. We also see `r-value` and `r-valueA`, which are the instantaneous and accumulated Lankford coefficients, respectively.  Let's plot these two as functions of tensile strain norm (column `eps_xx`):

<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_udsa_0_000_rvalues.png" alt="evolution of the r-values in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_udsa_45_000_rvalues.png" alt="evolution of the r-values in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_udsa_90_000_rvalues.png" alt="evolution of the r-values in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Evolution of the r-values in in UDSA uniaxial tensile tests (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

First of all, the instantaneous r-values evolve much faster. They also show more irregularities, since they are calculated based on instantaneous plastic strain rate. The accumulated r-value, which is derived from plastic strain (the integrated strain rate), evolve far slower.

It appears that the r-values in the TD directions follow a different evolution path than in the other two directions. That also means that \f$ \Delta r \f$ increases with deformation.

The tensile stresses are reported in the column `sigma_xx` as well:
<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_udsa_0_000_tensile_stress.png" alt="tensile stress in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_udsa_45_000_tensile_stress.png" alt="tensile stress in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_udsa_90_000_tensile_stress.png" alt="tensile stress in  (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Evolution of the tensile stress (no strain hardening law) in UDSA uniaxial tensile tests (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

That's weird. It seems that the samples at 45&deg; and 90&deg; initially _soften_ and then only slightly harden. Well, it's pretty normal given the fact we do not use any hardening model at all. This also means the hardening effect results exclusively from the texture evolution.

It takes just a few steps to include the hardening. First, let's make a copy of `sid1687f_udsa.cfg` and name it `sid1687f_hardening_udsa.cfg`. We need to make a slight modification to its generic section: 
-# modify the output prefix (we don't want to overwrite the results of the previous tests, do we?) and change it to: `sid1687f_hardening_udsa`
-# change the hardening parameters: 
    - Set the flag to use default hardening to: `False`
    - add a line below and put there: `2`
    - add yet another line below and put the following three values there: 
      `42.  1.4e-2  0.24`

We have just requested [Swift hardening law](@ref hardening_model) with the parameters 
\f$ \tau_0 = 42 \f$ MPa, \f$  \gamma_0 = 1.4e-2 \f$ and \f$  n = 0.24 \f$ (if you are curious where are these numbers coming from, you are welcome to read our [tutorial on calibrating hardening laws in VEF](@ref tutorial_swift)). 

After the modifications, the new input file should look as shown in the listings below:
\includelineno sid1687f_hardening_udsa.cfg

It's time to re-run the program on our modified input file:
<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC UDSA sid1687f_hardening_udsa.cfg
</pre>

Let's plot the tensile stresses now:
<div class="figure">
<img class="fullsize" src="sid1687f_hardening_udsa_tensile_stress.png" alt="evolution of the tensile stresses in tensile test (DX51D steel)">
<div class="figcaption">Tensile stress evolution in tensile tests with strain hardening taken into account (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>


We may now also check how the texture evolves in this tensile test. We have to first calculate ODFs from the discrete textures stored in the CUR files. The CUR files contain several blocks: one block per increment and the final texture in the last one. :
<pre class="consoleView">
cur2c --input=sid1687f_hardening_udsa_0_000.CUR --nblock=42 -o sid1687f_hardening_udsa_0_000_42.C
</pre>
The [`cur2c`](@ref cur2c) command produces a series of files: `sid1687f_hardening_udsa_1.C` to `sid1687f_hardening_udsa_42.C`. 
As we remember, the program calculated 41 increments, so the final texture will be in 42nd block. We can be more selective and request just the final one from the two other CUR files:
<pre class="consoleView">
cur2c --input=sid1687f_hardening_udsa_45_000.CUR --block=42 -o sid1687f_hardening_udsa_45_000_42.C
cur2c --input=sid1687f_hardening_udsa_90_000.CUR --block=42 -o sid1687f_hardening_udsa_90_000_42.C
</pre>

We can plot ODF sections using the last one, which corresponds to the final deformation:
<pre class="consoleView">
odfplot --input sid1687f_hardening_udsa_0_000_42.C  sid1687f_hardening_udsa_45_000_42.C sid1687f_hardening_udsa_90_000_42.C --style=filled --odfmin=0.9 --odfmax=6.0
</pre>
Here are the requested ODF sections:
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
	<img class="fullsize widefigure" src="sid1687f_hardening_udsa_0_000_42.png" alt="final deformation texture in UDSA tensile test (DX51D steel)">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">Deformation texture at tensile strain 0.4 predicted by the UDSA - direction 0&deg; wrt RD (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
	<img class="fullsize widefigure" src="sid1687f_hardening_udsa_45_000_42.png" alt="final deformation texture in UDSA tensile test (DX51D steel)">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">Deformation texture at tensile strain 0.4 predicted by the UDSA - direction 45&deg; wrt RD (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>
<div class="figure">
	<span class="zoom">
	<img class="fullsize widefigure" src="sid1687f_hardening_udsa_90_000_42.png" alt="final deformation texture in UDSA tensile test (DX51D steel)">
	</span>
	<div class="figcaption">Deformation texture at tensile strain 0.4 predicted by the UDSA - direction 90&deg; wrt RD (DX51D steel)</div>
</div>

It is immediately clear from these sections that the evolution of the texture greatly depends on the direction of the imposed tension. In-depth interpretation of the texture evolution is beyond the scope of this tutorial. Briefly speaking, the \f$ \gamma \f$-fibre breaks into individual components, but the changes are not limited to this effect.

Deformation under arbitrary stress state    {#gt_asr}
----------------------------------------

Obviously, the uniaxial stress state is not the only one that is relevant in simulating plastic deformations. In fact, quite the opposite is true: we often want to find out how the material performs under more complex state. We sometimes also want to know what happens as a result of a pre-deformation under some known stress mode.
  
Suppose we are interested how the material would behave if we deform it under a given stress state and subsequently impose another deformation, and possibly yet another deformation, and so on. The module ASR can do such calculation for us.

Let us get input file `sid1687f_asr.cfg` from `examples` directory: 

\includelineno sid1687f_asr.cfg

The essential part of the file (from line 14 onwards) instructs the `alamDMC` program to apply 4 consecutive deformation steps, each of different stress mode but identical plastic strain magnitude 0.2. So, let's run it:

<pre class="consoleView">
alamDMC ASR sid1687f_asr.cfg
</pre>

*/

